 {
  "id": "ukmt-pink-2018-21",
  "bank": "ukmt",
  "source": {
   "contest": "UKMT Pink Kangaroo",
   "paper": "2018",
   "year": 2018,
   "number": 21,
   "url": "https://ukmt.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/pink-2018-q.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "9-10",
  "points": 6,
  "blankPoints": null,
  "wrongPoints": null,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "An annulus is a shape made from two concentric circles. The diagram shows an annulus consisting of two concentric circles of radii 2 and 9. Inside this annulus two circles are drawn without overlapping, each being tangent to both of the concentric circles that make the annulus. In a different annulus made by concentric circles of radii 1 and 9, what would be the largest possible number of non-overlapping circles that could be drawn in this way?",
  "image": "qimages/ukmt/pink-2018/q21.webp",
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "2"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "3"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "4"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "5"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "6"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "B",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Radius of the circles",
    "body": "Each circle touches both boundary circles, so its diameter is $9-1=8$ and its radius is 4. Its centre is $1+4=5$ from the centre $O$ of the annulus.\n\nTwo neighbouring circles touch when their centres are 8 apart. In the triangle formed by $O$ and two such centres the sides are $5,5,8$, so by the cosine rule the angle $\\theta$ at $O$ satisfies $\\cos\\theta=\\frac{25+25-64}{50}=-0.28$, giving $\\theta\\approx106^\\circ$. Each circle therefore uses at least $106^\\circ$ of the way round, so at most $\\lfloor360/106.3\\rfloor=3$ circles fit, and three do fit since $3\\times106.3^\\circ<360^\\circ$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 3**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "geometry"
  ],
